<treenode node="clear;JS:Function Name;4218074545;f0;351;9;351;;;;vpos;;v;;aList;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Returns a new List with 0 size and no values in constant time.&#10;&lt;!-- runkit:activate&#10;{ &quot;preamble&quot;: &quot;const { List } = require(&apos;immutable&apos;);s&quot; }&#10;--&gt;&#10;```js&#10;List([ 1, 2, 3, 4 ]).clear()&#10;// List []&#10;```&#10;Note: `clear` can be used in `withMutations`.;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
</treenode>
<treenode node="delete;JS:Function Name;4218074545;f0;314;10;314;;;;vpos;;v;;aList;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Returns a new List which excludes this `index` and with a size 1 less&#10;than this List. Values at indices above `index` are shifted down by 1 to&#10;fill the position.&#10;This is synonymous with `list.splice(index, 1)`.&#10;`index` may be a negative number, which indexes back from the end of the&#10;List. `v.delete(-1)` deletes the last item in the List.&#10;Note: `delete` cannot be safely used in IE8&#10;&lt;!-- runkit:activate&#10;{ &quot;preamble&quot;: &quot;const { List } = require(&apos;immutable&apos;);s&quot; }&#10;--&gt;&#10;```js&#10;List([ 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 ]).delete(0);s&#10;// List [ 1, 2, 3, 4 ]&#10;```&#10;Since `delete()` re-indexes values, it produces a complete copy, which&#10;has `O(N)` complexity.&#10;Note: `delete` *cannot* be used in `withMutations`.&#10;&lt;b&gt;alias&lt;/b&gt;: remove;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
</treenode>
<treenode node="deleteIn;JS:Function Name;4218074545;f0;544;12;544;;;;vpos;;v;;athis;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Returns a new List having removed the value at this `keyPath`. If any&#10;keys in `keyPath` do not exist, no change will occur.&#10;&lt;!-- runkit:activate --&gt;&#10;```js&#10;const { List } = require(&apos;immutable&apos;)&#10;const list = List([ 0, 1, 2, List([ 3, 4 ])])&#10;list.deleteIn([3, 0]);s&#10;// List [ 0, 1, 2, List [ 4 ] ]&#10;```&#10;Plain JavaScript Object or Arrays may be nested within an Immutable.js&#10;Collection, and removeIn() can update those values as well, treating them&#10;immutably by creating new copies of those values with the changes applied.&#10;&lt;!-- runkit:activate --&gt;&#10;```js&#10;const { List } = require(&apos;immutable&apos;)&#10;const list = List([ 0, 1, 2, { plain: &apos;object&apos; }])&#10;list.removeIn([3, &apos;plain&apos;]);s&#10;// List([ 0, 1, 2, {}])&#10;```&#10;Note: `deleteIn` *cannot* be safely used in `withMutations`.&#10;&lt;b&gt;alias&lt;/b&gt;: removeIn;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;lkeyPath;;punknown;;pIterable;;x;;x;;n">
</treenode>
