<treenode node="mergeDeepIn;JS:Function Name;4218074545;f0;1437;15;1437;;;;vpos;;v;;athis;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    A combination of `updateIn` and `mergeDeep`, returning a new Map, but&#10;performing the deep merge at a point arrived at by following the keyPath.&#10;In other words, these two lines are equivalent:&#10;```js&#10;map.updateIn([&apos;a&apos;, &apos;b&apos;, &apos;c&apos;], abc =&gt; abc.mergeDeep(y))&#10;map.mergeDeepIn([&apos;a&apos;, &apos;b&apos;, &apos;c&apos;], y)&#10;```&#10;Note: `mergeDeepIn` can be used in `withMutations`.;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;lkeyPath;;punknown;;punknown;;pIterable;;x...collections Array;;x;;x;;n">
</treenode>
<treenode node="mergeDeepWith;JS:Function Name;4218074545;f0;1239;17;1239;;;;vpos;;v;;athis;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Like `mergeDeep()`, but when two non-collections or incompatible&#10;collections are encountered at the same key, it uses the `merger`&#10;function to determine the resulting value. Collections are considered&#10;incompatible if they fall into separate categories between keyed,&#10;indexed, and set-like.&#10;&lt;!-- runkit:activate --&gt;&#10;```js&#10;const { Map } = require(&apos;immutable&apos;)&#10;const one = Map({ a: Map({ x: 10, y: 10 }), b: Map({ x: 20, y: 50 }) })&#10;const two = Map({ a: Map({ x: 2 }), b: Map({ y: 5 }), c: Map({ z: 3 }) })&#10;one.mergeDeepWith((oldVal, newVal) =&gt; oldVal / newVal, two)&#10;// Map {&#10;//   &quot;a&quot;: Map { &quot;x&quot;: 5, &quot;y&quot;: 10 },&#10;//   &quot;b&quot;: Map { &quot;x&quot;: 20, &quot;y&quot;: 10 },&#10;//   &quot;c&quot;: Map { &quot;z&quot;: 3 }&#10;// }&#10;```&#10;Note: `mergeDeepWith` can be used in `withMutations`.;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;lmerger;;poldVal;;pnewVal;;pkey;;punknown;;pIterable;;pK;;pV;;pkey;;p(;;xunknown;;xunknown;;xunknown;;x...collections Array;;x;;x;;x;;xstring V;;x;;n">
</treenode>
<treenode node="mergeIn;JS:Function Name;4218074545;f0;1423;11;1423;;;;vpos;;v;;athis;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    A combination of `updateIn` and `merge`, returning a new Map, but&#10;performing the merge at a point arrived at by following the keyPath.&#10;In other words, these two lines are equivalent:&#10;```js&#10;map.updateIn([&apos;a&apos;, &apos;b&apos;, &apos;c&apos;], abc =&gt; abc.merge(y))&#10;map.mergeIn([&apos;a&apos;, &apos;b&apos;, &apos;c&apos;], y)&#10;```&#10;Note: `mergeIn` can be used in `withMutations`.;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;lkeyPath;;punknown;;punknown;;pIterable;;x...collections Array;;x;;x;;n">
</treenode>
