<treenode node="zip;JS:JS Var Name;4218074545;f0;2261;7;2261;;;;vpos;;v;;a;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Returns an OrderedSet of the same type &quot;zipped&quot; with the provided&#10;collections.&#10;Like `zipWith`, but using the default `zipper`: creating an `Array`.&#10;```js&#10;const a = OrderedSet([ 1, 2, 3 ])&#10;const b = OrderedSet([ 4, 5, 6 ])&#10;const c = a.zip(b)&#10;// OrderedSet [ [ 1, 4 ], [ 2, 5 ], [ 3, 6 ] ]&#10;```;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
</treenode>
<treenode node="zipAll;JS:JS Var Name;4218074545;f0;2287;10;2287;;;;vpos;;v;;a;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Returns a OrderedSet of the same type &quot;zipped&quot; with the provided&#10;collections.&#10;Unlike `zip`, `zipAll` continues zipping until the longest collection is&#10;exhausted. Missing values from shorter collections are filled with `undefined`.&#10;```js&#10;const a = OrderedSet([ 1, 2 ]);s&#10;const b = OrderedSet([ 3, 4, 5 ]);s&#10;const c = a.zipAll(b);s // OrderedSet [ [ 1, 3 ], [ 2, 4 ], [ undefined, 5 ] ]&#10;```&#10;Note: Since zipAll will return a collection as large as the largest&#10;input, some results may contain undefined values. TypeScript cannot&#10;account for these without cases (as of v2.5).;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
</treenode>
<treenode node="zipWith;JS:JS Var Name;4218074545;f0;2302;11;2302;;;;vpos;;v;;a;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Returns an OrderedSet of the same type &quot;zipped&quot; with the provided&#10;collections by using a custom `zipper` function.&#10;&lt;b&gt;see&lt;/b&gt;: Seq.Indexed.zipWith;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
</treenode>
</treenode>
<treenode node="Seq;TS:Interface Name;4218074545;f2;3552;3;3677;;;;vposV;;v;;aCollection;;c;;r;;d47,3552;;b0,3;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
<treenode node="cacheResult;JS:Function Name;4218074545;f0;3587;15;3587;;;;vpos;;v;;athis;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Because Sequences are lazy and designed to be chained together, they do&#10;not cache their results. For example, this map function is called a total&#10;of 6 times, as each `join` iterates the Seq of three values.&#10;var squares = Seq([ 1, 2, 3 ]).map(x =&gt; x * x)&#10;squares.join() + squares.join()&#10;If you know a `Seq` will be used multiple times, it may be more&#10;efficient to first cache it in memory. Here, the map function is called&#10;only 3 times.&#10;var squares = Seq([ 1, 2, 3 ]).map(x =&gt; x * x).cacheResult()&#10;squares.join() + squares.join()&#10;Use this method judiciously, as it must fully evaluate a Seq which can be&#10;a burden on memory and possibly performance.&#10;Note: after calling `cacheResult`, a Seq will always have a `size`.;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
</treenode>
