<treenode node="concat;JS:JS Var Name;4218074545;f0;4237;12;4237;;;;vpos;;v;;a;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Returns a new Collection with other collections concatenated to this one.;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
</treenode>
<treenode node="context;JS:JS Var Name;4218074545;f0;4253;15;4253;;;;vposC;;v;;a;;r;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
</treenode>
<treenode node="filter;JS:JS Var Name;4218074545;f0;4273;12;4273;;;;vpos;;v;;a;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Returns a new Collection with only the values for which the `predicate`&#10;function returns true.&#10;Note: `filter()` always returns a new instance, even if it results in&#10;not filtering out any values.;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
</treenode>
<treenode node="flatMap;JS:JS Var Name;4218074545;f0;4261;13;4261;;;;vpos;;v;;a;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Flat-maps the Collection, returning a Collection of the same type.&#10;Similar to `collection.map(...).flatten(true)`.;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
</treenode>
<treenode node="map;JS:JS Var Name;4218074545;f0;4251;9;4251;;;;vpos;;v;;a;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Returns a new Collection.Set with values passed through a&#10;`mapper` function.&#10;```&#10;Collection.Set([ 1, 2 ]).map(x =&gt; 10 * x)&#10;// Seq { 1, 2 }&#10;```&#10;Note: `map()` always returns a new instance, even if it produced the&#10;same value at every step.;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
</treenode>
<treenode node="partition;JS:JS Var Name;4218074545;f0;4287;15;4287;;;;vpos;;v;;a;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Returns a new set Collection with the values for which the&#10;`predicate` function returns false and another for which is returns&#10;true.;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
</treenode>
</treenode>
<treenode node="Indexed;TS:NameSpace Name;4218074545;f4;3887;24;3887;;;;vpos;;v;;a;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Definition&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    namespace Indexed&#10;&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Indexed Collections have incrementing numeric keys. They exhibit&#10;slightly different behavior than `Collection.Keyed` for some methods in order&#10;to better mirror the behavior of JavaScript&apos;s `Array`, and add methods&#10;which do not make sense on non-indexed Collections such as `indexOf`.&#10;Unlike JavaScript arrays, `Collection.Indexed`s are always dense. &quot;Unset&quot;&#10;indices and `undefined` indices are indistinguishable, and all indices from&#10;0 to `size` are visited when iterated.&#10;All Collection.Indexed methods return re-indexed Collections. In other words,&#10;indices always start at 0 and increment until size. If you wish to&#10;preserve indices, using them as keys, convert to a Collection.Keyed by&#10;calling `toKeyedSeq`.;;d22,3887;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
</treenode>
<treenode node="Keyed;TS:NameSpace Name;4218074545;f4;3701;22;3701;;;;vpos;;v;;a;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Definition&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    namespace Keyed&#10;&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Keyed Collections have discrete keys tied to each value.&#10;When iterating `Collection.Keyed`, each iteration will yield a `[K, V]`&#10;tuple, in other words, `Collection#entries` is the default iterator for&#10;Keyed Collections.;;d20,3701;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
</treenode>
<treenode node="Set;TS:NameSpace Name;4218074545;f4;4200;20;4200;;;;vpos;;v;;a;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Definition&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    namespace Set&#10;&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Set Collections only represent values. They have no associated keys or&#10;indices. Duplicate values are possible in the lazy `Seq.Set`s, however&#10;the concrete `Set` Collection does not allow duplicate values.&#10;Collection methods on Collection.Set such as `map` and `forEach` will provide&#10;the value as both the first and second arguments to the provided function.&#10;```js&#10;const { Collection } = require(&apos;immutable&apos;)&#10;const seq = Collection.Set([ &apos;A&apos;, &apos;B&apos;, &apos;C&apos; ])&#10;// Seq { &quot;A&quot;, &quot;B&quot;, &quot;C&quot; }&#10;seq.forEach((v, k) =&gt;&#10;assert.equal(v, k)&#10;)&#10;```;;d18,4200;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
</treenode>
<treenode node="Indexed;JS:Function Name;4218074545;f0;3895;20;3895;;;;vposT;;v;;aCollection.Indexed;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Creates a new Collection.Indexed.&#10;Note: `Collection.Indexed` is a conversion function and not a class, and&#10;does not use the `new` keyword during construction.;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;lcollection;;pT;;pArrayLike;;pT;;poptional Iterable;;x;;x;;x;;x;;n">
</treenode>
<treenode node="Keyed;JS:Function Name;4218074545;f0;3712;18;3712;;;;vposV;;v;;aCollection.Keyed;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Creates a Collection.Keyed&#10;Similar to `Collection()`, however it expects collection-likes of [K, V]&#10;tuples if not constructed from a Collection.Keyed or JS Object.&#10;Note: `Collection.Keyed` is a conversion function and not a class, and&#10;does not use the `new` keyword during construction.;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;lobj;;pkey;;p{;;xstring V;;x;;n">
</treenode>
<treenode node="Set;JS:Function Name;4218074545;f0;4208;16;4208;;;;vposT;;v;;aCollection.Set;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Similar to `Collection()`, but always returns a Collection.Set.&#10;Note: `Collection.Set` is a factory function and not a class, and does&#10;not use the `new` keyword during construction.;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;lcollection;;pT;;pArrayLike;;pT;;poptional Iterable;;x;;x;;x;;x;;n">
</treenode>
</treenode>
<treenode node="Factory;TS:NameSpace Name;4218074545;f4;2836;24;2836;;;;vpos;;v;;a;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Definition&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    namespace Factory&#10;&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    A Record.Factory is created by the `Record()` function. Record instances&#10;are created by passing it some of the accepted values for that Record&#10;type:&#10;&lt;!-- runkit:activate&#10;{ &quot;preamble&quot;: &quot;const { Record } = require(&apos;immutable&apos;)&quot; }&#10;--&gt;&#10;```js&#10;// makePerson is a Record Factory function&#10;const makePerson = Record({ name: null, favoriteColor: &apos;unknown&apos; });s&#10;// alan is a Record instance&#10;const alan = makePerson({ name: &apos;Alan&apos; });s&#10;```&#10;Note that Record Factories return `Record&lt;TProps&gt; &amp; Readonly&lt;TProps&gt;`,&#10;this allows use of both the Record instance API, and direct property&#10;access on the resulting instances:&#10;&lt;!-- runkit:activate&#10;{ &quot;preamble&quot;: &quot;const { Record } = require(&apos;immutable&apos;);sconst makePerson = Record({ name: null, favoriteColor: &apos;unknown&apos; });sconst alan = makePerson({ name: &apos;Alan&apos; });s&quot; }&#10;--&gt;&#10;```js&#10;// Use the Record API&#10;console.log(&apos;Record API: &apos; + alan.get(&apos;name&apos;))&#10;// Or direct property access (Readonly)&#10;console.log(&apos;property access: &apos; + alan.name)&#10;```&#10;**Flow Typing Records:**&#10;Use the `RecordFactory&lt;TProps&gt;` Flow type to get high quality type checking of&#10;Records:&#10;```js&#10;import type { RecordFactory, RecordOf } from &apos;immutable&apos;;s&#10;// Use RecordFactory&lt;TProps&gt; for defining new Record factory functions.&#10;type PersonProps = { name: ?string, favoriteColor: string };s&#10;const makePerson: RecordFactory&lt;PersonProps&gt; = Record({ name: null, favoriteColor: &apos;unknown&apos; });s&#10;// Use RecordOf&lt;T&gt; for defining new instances of that Record.&#10;type Person = RecordOf&lt;PersonProps&gt;;s&#10;const alan: Person = makePerson({ name: &apos;Alan&apos; });s&#10;```;;d22,2836;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
</treenode>
<treenode node="List;TS:NameSpace Name;4218074545;f2;180;3;213;;;;vpos;;v;;a;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Definition&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    namespace List&#10;&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Lists are ordered indexed dense collections, much like a JavaScript&#10;Array.&#10;Lists are immutable and fully persistent with O(log32 N) gets and sets,&#10;and O(1) push and pop.&#10;Lists implement Deque, with efficient addition and removal from both the&#10;end (`push`, `pop`) and beginning (`unshift`, `shift`).&#10;Unlike a JavaScript Array, there is no distinction between an&#10;&quot;unset&quot; index and an index set to `undefined`. `List#forEach` visits all&#10;indices from 0 to size, regardless of whether they were explicitly defined.;;d17,180;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
<treenode node="isList;JS:Function Name;4218074545;f0;191;19;191;;;;vpos;;v;;aList;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    True if the provided value is a List&#10;&lt;!-- runkit:activate --&gt;&#10;```js&#10;const { List } = require(&apos;immutable&apos;);s&#10;List.isList([]);s // false&#10;List.isList(List());s // true&#10;```;;d-1,-1;;b0,5;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;lmaybeList;;punknown;;x;;n">
</treenode>
<treenode node="of;JS:Function Name;4218074545;f0;212;15;212;;;;vposT;;v;;aList;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Creates a new List containing `values`.&#10;&lt;!-- runkit:activate --&gt;&#10;```js&#10;const { List } = require(&apos;immutable&apos;);s&#10;List.of(1, 2, 3, 4)&#10;// List [ 1, 2, 3, 4 ]&#10;```&#10;Note: Values are not altered or converted in any way.&#10;&lt;!-- runkit:activate --&gt;&#10;```js&#10;const { List } = require(&apos;immutable&apos;);s&#10;List.of({x:1}, 2, [3], 4)&#10;// List [ { x: 1 }, 2, [ 3 ], 4 ]&#10;```;;d-1,-1;;b0,5;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;lT;;p;;x;;n">
</treenode>
</treenode>
<treenode node="Map;TS:NameSpace Name;4218074545;f2;782;3;794;;;;vpos;;v;;a;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Definition&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    namespace Map&#10;&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Immutable Map is an unordered Collection.Keyed of (key, value) pairs with&#10;`O(log32 N)` gets and `O(log32 N)` persistent sets.&#10;Iteration order of a Map is undefined, however is stable. Multiple&#10;iterations of the same Map will iterate in the same order.&#10;Map&apos;s keys can be of any type, and use `Immutable.is` to determine key&#10;equality. This allows the use of any value (including NaN) as a key.&#10;Because `Immutable.is` returns equality based on value semantics, and&#10;Immutable collections are treated as values, any Immutable collection may&#10;be used as a key.&#10;&lt;!-- runkit:activate --&gt;&#10;```js&#10;const { Map, List } = require(&apos;immutable&apos;);s&#10;Map().set(List([ 1 ]), &apos;listofone&apos;).get(List([ 1 ]));s&#10;// &apos;listofone&apos;&#10;```&#10;Any JavaScript object may be used as a key, however strict identity is used&#10;to evaluate key equality. Two similar looking objects will represent two&#10;different keys.&#10;Implemented by a hash-array mapped trie.;;d16,782;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
<treenode node="isMap;JS:Function Name;4218074545;f0;793;18;793;;;;vpos;;v;;aMap;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    True if the provided value is a Map&#10;&lt;!-- runkit:activate --&gt;&#10;```js&#10;const { Map } = require(&apos;immutable&apos;)&#10;Map.isMap({}) // false&#10;Map.isMap(Map()) // true&#10;```;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;lmaybeMap;;punknown;;x;;n">
</treenode>
</treenode>
<treenode node="OrderedMap;TS:NameSpace Name;4218074545;f2;1665;3;1672;;;;vpos;;v;;a;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Definition&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    namespace OrderedMap&#10;&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    A type of Map that has the additional guarantee that the iteration order of&#10;entries will be the order in which they were set().&#10;The iteration behavior of OrderedMap is the same as native ES6 Map and&#10;JavaScript Object.&#10;Note that `OrderedMap` are more expensive than non-ordered `Map` and may&#10;consume more memory. `OrderedMap#set` is amortized O(log32 N), but not&#10;stable.;;d23,1665;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
<treenode node="isOrderedMap;JS:Function Name;4218074545;f0;1669;25;1669;;;;vpos;;v;;aOrderedMap;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    True if the provided value is an OrderedMap.;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;lmaybeOrderedMap;;punknown;;x;;n">
</treenode>
</treenode>
<treenode node="OrderedSet;TS:NameSpace Name;4218074545;f2;2144;3;2164;;;;vpos;;v;;a;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Definition&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    namespace OrderedSet&#10;&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    A type of Set that has the additional guarantee that the iteration order of&#10;values will be the order in which they were `add`ed.&#10;The iteration behavior of OrderedSet is the same as native ES6 Set.&#10;Note that `OrderedSet` are more expensive than non-ordered `Set` and may&#10;consume more memory. `OrderedSet#add` is amortized O(log32 N), but not&#10;stable.;;d23,2144;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
<treenode node="fromKeys;JS:Function Name;4218074545;f0;2161;21;2161;;;;vposT;;v;;aOrderedSet;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    `OrderedSet.fromKeys()` creates a new immutable OrderedSet containing&#10;the keys from this Collection or JavaScript Object.;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;lobj;;pkey;;p{[;;xstring] unknown;;x;;n">
</treenode>
<treenode node="isOrderedSet;JS:Function Name;4218074545;f0;2148;25;2148;;;;vpos;;v;;aOrderedSet;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    True if the provided value is an OrderedSet.;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;lmaybeOrderedSet;;punknown;;x;;n">
</treenode>
<treenode node="of;JS:Function Name;4218074545;f0;2155;15;2155;;;;vposT;;v;;aOrderedSet;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    Creates a new OrderedSet containing `values`.;;d-1,-1;;b0,0;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;lT;;p;;x;;n">
</treenode>
</treenode>
<treenode node="Seq;TS:NameSpace Name;4218074545;f2;3089;3;3522;;;;vpos;;v;;a;;r&#10;&lt;b&gt;Definition&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    namespace Seq&#10;&#10;&lt;b&gt;Description&lt;/b&gt;&#10;    `Seq` describes a lazy operation, allowing them to efficiently chain&#10;use of all the higher-order collection methods (such as `map` and `filter`)&#10;by not creating intermediate collections.&#10;**Seq is immutable** — Once a Seq is created, it cannot be&#10;changed, appended to, rearranged or otherwise modified. Instead, any&#10;mutative method called on a `Seq` will return a new `Seq`.&#10;**Seq is lazy** — `Seq` does as little work as necessary to respond to any&#10;method call. Values are often created during iteration, including implicit&#10;iteration when reducing or converting to a concrete data structure such as&#10;a `List` or JavaScript `Array`.&#10;For example, the following performs no work, because the resulting&#10;`Seq`&apos;s values are never iterated:&#10;```js&#10;const { Seq } = require(&apos;immutable&apos;)&#10;const oddSquares = Seq([ 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 ])&#10;.filter(x =&gt; x % 2 !== 0)&#10;.map(x =&gt; x * x)&#10;```&#10;Once the `Seq` is used, it performs only the work necessary. In this&#10;example, no intermediate arrays are ever created, filter is called three&#10;times, and map is only called once:&#10;```js&#10;oddSquares.get(1);s // 9&#10;```&#10;Any collection can be converted to a lazy Seq with `Seq()`.&#10;&lt;!-- runkit:activate --&gt;&#10;```js&#10;const { Map } = require(&apos;immutable&apos;)&#10;const map = Map({ a: 1, b: 2, c: 3 })&#10;const lazySeq = Seq(map)&#10;```&#10;`Seq` allows for the efficient chaining of operations, allowing for the&#10;expression of logic that can otherwise be very tedious:&#10;```js&#10;lazySeq&#10;.flip()&#10;.map(key =&gt; key.toUpperCase())&#10;.flip()&#10;// Seq { A: 1, B: 1, C: 1 }&#10;```&#10;As well as expressing logic that would otherwise seem memory or time&#10;limited, for example `Range` is a special kind of Lazy sequence.&#10;&lt;!-- runkit:activate --&gt;&#10;```js&#10;const { Range } = require(&apos;immutable&apos;)&#10;Range(1, Infinity)&#10;.skip(1000)&#10;.map(n =&gt; -n)&#10;.filter(n =&gt; n % 2 === 0)&#10;.take(2)&#10;.reduce((r, n) =&gt; r * n, 1)&#10;// 1006008&#10;```&#10;Seq is often used to provide a rich collection API to JavaScript Object.&#10;```js&#10;Seq({ x: 0, y: 1, z: 2 }).map(v =&gt; v * 2).toObject();s&#10;// { x: 0, y: 2, z: 4 }&#10;```;;d16,3089;;b0,3;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
<treenode node="Indexed;TS:Interface Name;4218074545;f4;3260;21;3260;;;;vposT;;v;;aSeq;;c;;r;;d-1,-1;;b0,3;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
</treenode>
<treenode node="Keyed;TS:Interface Name;4218074545;f4;3116;19;3116;;;;vposV;;v;;aSeq;;c;;r;;d-1,-1;;b0,3;;m0;;e0;;i0;;o;;k;;l;;n">
</treenode>
